{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's 'strategic imperative' to maximize error elimination collapses into the very induction Popper rejected when applied to the specific context of 'choosing which theory to act on' for future engineering safety. The target argues that choosing the best-corroborated theory is a rational bet for the 'next immediate engagement' without guaranteeing future success. However, acting on a theory for safety (e.g., building a bridge) inherently requires the assumption that the theory's past survival predicts its future reliability. If one truly accepts the target's premise that corroboration offers 'no guarantee of future success,' then acting on the better-corroborated theory over a worse one is logically arbitrary regarding future outcomes. To claim it is the 'only rational bet' smuggles in the inductive principle that the future will resemble the past (specifically, that the mechanism of falsification will continue to operate similarly), which is precisely the inference Popper sought to avoid. The target fails to distinguish between a methodological rule for testing (do not stop trying to falsify) and a pragmatic rule for action (trust this theory to hold), where the latter cannot be defended without an inductive bridge.","premise":"Acting on a theory for future safety requires assuming past performance predicts future reliability.","successor_question":"Can a non-inductive strategic imperative justify preferring Theory A over Theory B for a specific future action if both are logically equally likely to be false tomorrow?","target":"fb875e79f53d5e5004db076687917d8befe989ba396a5f718087604814cdd1c8"}]}